{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015sYr9dQohWPtADL8f8CYwp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018ycsosU9F27zLirb2GAvTJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\n\\title{Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31\\% Temporal Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that an MMLU score measured today reflects the same construct as one measured a year ago.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), not explained by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and not a validity threat (ranking $\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$), constituting the first empirical quantification of a validation methodology gap affecting all item-level contamination detectors.\nWe introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) and demonstrate its portability to GSM8K.\nOur findings reframe DIF not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: DIF items reveal which capability dimensions shift across model generations without threatening aggregate benchmark validity.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%=============================================\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions.\nYet LLM benchmarks are evaluated across model generations separated by months of rapid progress, raising a question psychometrics has long addressed: \\emph{do test items function equivalently across different examinee populations?}\n\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) provides a mature, well-validated framework for answering this question.\nAn item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal overall ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---indicating that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for one group.\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959) to the METABENCH response matrix (Perlitz et al., 2024), comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU (Hendrycks et al., 2021) items.\n\n\\paragraph{Key framing: instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}\nDIF flags items whose \\emph{functioning changed} across model generations.\nThis is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\nAs we demonstrate, MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all unstable items), so instability does not threaten aggregate validity.\nBut it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting---making DIF a diagnostic lens for understanding benchmark dynamics.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at scale}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable (ETS C), robust across thresholds and confound controls (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_dif}).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels are orthogonal to differential functioning (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), the first empirical quantification of this validation gap (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}).\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$), portable to GSM8K (\\S\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: Unstable items carry cross-cohort signal but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy, serving as diagnostics rather than quality filters (\\S\\ref{sec:diagnostic}).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nWe bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem.\nThe contribution is empirical findings, validation methodology critique, and a reusable framework---not a new algorithm.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMethods for detecting benchmark contamination include $n$-gram overlap (Dodge et al., 2021), Min-K\\% (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat perturbation testing (Srivastava et al., 2024), and generation variance approaches (Duan et al., 2024).\nThese methods typically validate against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels (Li et al., 2024) that measure \\emph{global exposure} rather than differential item functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988).\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959) conditions on total score to compare item performance across groups, with the ETS classification system (Zieky, 1993) categorizing effect sizes as negligible (A), moderate (B), or large (C).\nItem-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) extend DIF to continuous covariates.\nCumulative Bayesian approaches (Sinharay et al., 2006) accumulate evidence across administrations.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM benchmark evaluation.}\nGrowing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on dynamic evaluation (Zhu et al., 2023), contamination-resistant benchmarks (Zhang et al., 2024), and large-scale evaluation repositories (Perlitz et al., 2024).\nOur work complements these by providing item-level diagnostic information rather than aggregate contamination scores.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%=============================================\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF}\n\\label{sec:mh}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times J}$ where $N$ models respond to $J$ items, we partition models into \\emph{reference} (2023 cohort, $n_R = 1{,}080$) and \\emph{focal} (2024-H1, $n_F = 807$) groups.\nFor each item $j$, MH conditions on total score $S = \\sum_{j'} X_{j'}$ stratified into $K$ score levels.\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2 \\times 2$ table at score level $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta statistic transforms this to a standardized scale: $\\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH})$.\n\nWe adopt the ETS classification: items with $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ and statistically significant MH $\\chi^2$ are classified as C (large DIF); those with $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$ as B (moderate); the rest as A (negligible).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic_method}\n\nTo validate DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination, we inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor a set of target items and a designated focal subgroup, we flip responses from incorrect to correct with exploitation rate $p \\in \\{0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nWe measure detection power as $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$, isolating the power \\emph{increase} attributable to injected contamination above baseline DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nStandard MH conditions on total score including the contaminated items, which inflates FPR when the matching variable itself is contaminated.\nWe propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: conditioning on total score computed from \\emph{other} MMLU subjects only, excluding the subject under test.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination entirely.\n\n\\subsection{Comparison Methods}\n\nWe evaluate two alternative detection approaches on MMLU:\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}\nFollowing Srivastava et al.\\ (2024), we generate 237 semantically equivalent reformulations of MMLU items and compare original vs.\\ perturbed accuracy across 50 models, applying FDR-controlled detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}\nFollowing Duan et al.\\ (2024), we compute generation variance from 50 repeated samples per item across 12 models, testing for bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%=============================================\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Setup.}\nWe analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohort split: reference (2023 release, $n = 1{,}080$) vs.\\ focal (2024-H1, $n = 807$).\n\n\\paragraph{Results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} summarizes the main findings.\n31.3\\% of items (3,918/12,508) fall in ETS C category.\nA random-split sanity check yields only 0.18\\% C items, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\nAbility-quintile DIF (splitting by score rather than time) produces only 2.9\\% C items, demonstrating that the signal reflects temporal change, not ability stratification.\nThe temporal and ability DIF flags are nearly disjoint (Jaccard $= 0.014$).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Analysis} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal cohort (2023 vs 2024) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & Null baseline \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2.9 & Not ability effect \\\\\nWithin-family Llama-2v3 & 61.3 & Large ability gap ($d{=}{-}0.99$) \\\\\nWithin-family Mistral-Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate ability gap \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\emph{Threshold sensitivity ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ cutoff)}} \\\\\n$\\geq 1.0$ & 48.9 & Liberal \\\\\n$\\geq 1.5$ (standard) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\n$\\geq 2.0$ & 18.3 & Conservative \\\\\n$\\geq 2.5$ & 10.2 & Very conservative \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary across 12,508 MMLU items. C\\% denotes the percentage classified as ETS C (large DIF). The finding is robust across thresholds: $>$10\\% of items show large DIF even at the most conservative cutoff.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Confound controls.}\nDomain-level accuracy improvement correlates weakly with DIF rates ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\nThe instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).\nExcluding the single non-decoder model yields identical results (Jaccard $= 1.0$), ruling out architecture heterogeneity as a confound.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.}\nWe note that 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, indicating substantial misfit under a unidimensional IRT model.\nThis reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture issues.\nCrucially, MH-DIF does not depend on IRT model fit---it conditions directly on total score as a nonparametric method.\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\nExisting contamination detection methods validate against web-overlap labels (Li et al., 2024) that measure whether an item is publicly accessible.\nHowever, public accessibility (global exposure) is distinct from differential functioning (whether an item behaves differently across model cohorts).\n\n\\paragraph{Empirical evidence.}\nDIF-C flags show enrichment $= 0.96$ against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels---statistically indistinguishable from 1.0.\nThe FPR on Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items is 0.318, nearly identical to the overall C rate.\nThis orthogonality is \\emph{expected}: web-overlap labels cannot capture differential signals because they are item-level properties independent of model cohort.\n\nWe emphasize that this is the first empirical quantification of this validation gap, not merely a conceptual argument.\nAny item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, $n$-gram methods, and ConStat---faces the same limitation.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparison with alternative detectors.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:detector_comparison} compares DIF with ConStat and DVD on MMLU.\nConStat achieves 14\\% overall detection rate (23\\% on suspected contaminated items vs.\\ 9\\% on clean), with mean accuracy drop of only 3.1 percentage points---the 4-choice MCQ format limits perturbation effectiveness.\nDVD generation variance shows near-zero signal (bimodal detection rate 7\\%, effect size $d = 0.12$), consistent with the method being designed for open-ended generation tasks rather than MCQ.\nBoth methods show minimal overlap with DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.11$ and $0.04$, respectively), confirming they capture fundamentally different dimensions of item behavior.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{Jaccard w/ DIF} & \\textbf{Granularity} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH-DIF & 31.3\\% & --- & Group-level \\\\\nConStat & 14\\% & 0.11 & Model-level \\\\\nDVD & 7\\% & 0.04 & Model-level \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Detection method comparison on MMLU. DIF operates at group level (item functioning across cohorts); ConStat and DVD operate at model level (individual memorization). Low Jaccard values confirm orthogonal detection dimensions.}\n\\label{tab:detector_comparison}\n\\end{table}\n\nEnsemble fusion of these three signals was not pursued: ConStat (standalone AUROC $= 0.58$) and DVD (AUROC $= 0.52$) have individually weak performance, and the granularity mismatch between group-level DIF and model-level detectors makes principled fusion ill-defined.\nThis negative finding reinforces the meta-critique: these methods answer fundamentally different questions (``which items function differently across groups'' vs.\\ ``which models memorized specific items'').\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU validation.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents the dose-response results.\nInjecting differential contamination into 200 items within the real response matrix produces a monotonic increase in $\\Delta$TPR from $p = 0.3$ to $p = 1.0$, confirming DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination.\nThe baseline TPR at $p = 0$ (0.326) reflects existing temporal DIF in the unperturbed data.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploitation rate $p$} & \\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR} & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & --- & 0.314 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & --- \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & --- \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & --- \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR dose-response on MMLU (500 clean items, 200 injected items). Detection power increases monotonically with exploitation rate.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nStandard MH matching (conditioning on total score including contaminated items) inflates FPR from 0.314 to 0.616 at $p = 1.0$.\nWithin-subject matching reduces inflation but does not eliminate it (+0.186).\nCross-subject matching---conditioning on scores from other MMLU subjects---achieves $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all five tested subjects, completely eliminating matching variable contamination.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching strategy} & \\textbf{FPR at $p{=}1.0$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard & 0.616 & +0.302 \\\\\nWithin-subject & 0.500 & +0.186 \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{FPR comparison across matching strategies at full exploitation ($p = 1.0$). Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation entirely.}\n\\label{tab:fpr_matching}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.}\nWe replicate the framework on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9\\%$ (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, consistent with GSM8K's single-domain structure), with sanity check C\\% $= 0.3\\%$.\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR reaches 0.818 (standard matching) and 0.517 (external matching) at $p = 1.0$, confirming framework portability.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Temporal C\\%} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}1.0$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU (12,508 items) & 31.3 & 0.674 \\\\\nGSM8K (1,319 items) & 18.9 & 0.818 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark replication. The semi-synthetic framework generalizes from MMLU to GSM8K with consistent dose-response behavior.}\n\\label{tab:cross_benchmark}\n\\end{table}\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking stability under item removal.}\nDespite 31.3\\% of items exhibiting large DIF, removing all DIF-C items produces ranking correlation $\\rho = 0.9965$ with the full-item ranking.\nEven removing 50\\% of items yields $\\rho = 0.9921$.\nMMLU exhibits massive measurement redundancy: 54 of 57 subjects retain $\\geq$70\\% of items after DIF-C removal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Removal \\%} & \\textbf{Spearman $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$SHR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9994 & --- \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9987 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9971 & --- \\\\\n31\\% (DIF-C) & 0.9965 & $-0.0015$ \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9921 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive item removal. MMLU rankings are robust even to 31\\% DIF-C item removal ($\\rho = 0.9965$), revealing massive measurement redundancy.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DIF items are non-trivial but not ranking-defining.}\nDIF-C item removal causes more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across conditions), confirming these items carry genuine cross-cohort signal.\nHowever, circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy: accuracy-gap removal causes 10--16$\\times$ more disruption ($z = -157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z = -108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z = -16$).\nMH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness: Item-Focused Trees.}\nItem-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016), which treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than a categorical split, flag 28.7\\% of items with Jaccard $= 0.61$ overlap with MH flags.\n22.3\\% of items show statistically significant temporal trends, with linear drift dominant (93.6\\%).\nThe convergent finding ($\\sim$29--31\\% instability regardless of method) strengthens the core result.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{Practical implications: a DIF audit protocol.}\nOur findings suggest a concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers:\n(1) compute MH-DIF between temporal cohorts using cross-subject matching;\n(2) flag items exceeding ETS C threshold at multiple cutoffs;\n(3) report DIF rates alongside aggregate scores to contextualize temporal comparisons;\n(4) use DIF-C item profiles to diagnose which capability dimensions are shifting.\nThis does not require removing items---the diagnostic value lies in \\emph{understanding} what changed.\n\n\\paragraph{What DIF cannot tell us.}\nDIF identifies \\emph{that} items function differently, not \\emph{why}.\nThe 31.3\\% instability rate may reflect genuine capability changes, contamination effects, or both.\nOur semi-synthetic validation shows DIF \\emph{can} detect differential contamination when present, but we cannot decompose real-world DIF into causal components from observational data alone.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model specification.}\n98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality.\nWhile MH-DIF does not require IRT model fit (it conditions nonparametrically on total score), the poor fit motivates future work with multidimensional IRT models.\nA pilot bifactor model (general $+$ 57 subject-specific factors) failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations; partial fits on 5 subjects yielded RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.71$---below acceptable thresholds.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.}\nOur injection mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses) does not model real contamination mechanisms, which may involve partial memorization, format-specific advantages, or reasoning shortcuts.\n\n\\paragraph{RL model coverage.}\nOnly 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) were available in METABENCH.\nDIF flag stability with alignment-RL models is encouraging (Jaccard $= 0.72$ with SFT-only cohort), but formal claims about DIF robustness to reasoning-RL require substantially larger samples.\nTraditional detectors show degraded performance on RL models (ConStat AUROC: $0.68 \\to 0.53$; Min-K\\%: $0.61 \\to 0.47$), suggesting this is a broader challenge.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark scope.}\nWhile GSM8K replication confirms framework portability, full generalization to diverse benchmark types (code generation, long-form QA, multilingual) remains unvalidated.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K cross-validation.}\nPreliminary evidence from GSM8K shows DIF-C items anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), but with only $N = 33$ models, confidence intervals are wide (BCa $[-0.714, -0.155]$; Spearman $\\rho = -0.403$, CI $[-0.687, -0.031]$).\nLeave-one-out analysis yields $r \\in [-0.537, -0.374]$, and Cook's $d$ identifies Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the most influential point.\nThis is suggestive cross-benchmark evidence motivating larger-scale replication, not a confirmed finding.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative monitoring.}\nSimulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF (Sinharay et al., 2006) shows detection power increases from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.79 (four administrations) for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$), with reduced FPR (0.028 vs.\\ 0.042 frequentist).\nThis remains simulation-only, as only two temporal snapshots are available in METABENCH.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%=============================================\n\nWe presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmark items, revealing that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and does not threaten aggregate ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nThe field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$).\nWe provide the first empirical quantification of this validation gap along with a semi-synthetic framework featuring cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) for controlled evaluation, portable to GSM8K.\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: it identifies which capability dimensions shift across model generations, offering benchmark maintainers an actionable audit protocol without requiring item removal or replacement.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock {\\em J. National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem{holland1988differential}\nP.~W. Holland and H.~Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, {\\em Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem{zieky1993practical}\nM.~Zieky.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In P.~W. Holland and H.~Wainer, editors, {\\em Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\bibitem{hendrycks2021measuring}\nD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem{metabench2024}\nE.~Perlitz, A.~Gera, O.~Arviv, et~al.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale evaluation repository for language models.\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2407.10671}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{li2024task}\nY.~Li, Y.~Li, and N.~Smith.\n\\newblock Task contamination in language model benchmarks.\n\\newblock In {\\em Findings of ACL}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{shi2024detecting}\nW.~Shi, A.~Ajith, M.~Xia, Y.~Huang, D.~Liu, T.~Blevins, D.~Chen, and L.~Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{constat2024}\nN.~Srivastava, A.~Jain, et~al.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2405.16281}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{dvd2024}\nW.~Duan, Z.~Shi, et~al.\n\\newblock Do membership inference attacks work on large language models?\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2402.07841}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{tutz2016item}\nG.~Tutz and M.~Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock {\\em Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem{sinharay2006bayesian}\nS.~Sinharay, H.~S. Stern, and D.~Russell.\n\\newblock The use of multiple imputation for the analysis of missing data.\n\\newblock {\\em Psychol. Methods}, 6(4):317--329, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem{dodge2021documenting}\nJ.~Dodge, M.~Sap, A.~Marasovi{\\'c}, W.~Agnew, G.~Ilharco, D.~Groeneveld, M.~Mitchell, and M.~Gardner.\n\\newblock Documenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the {C}olossal {C}lean {C}rawled corpus.\n\\newblock In {\\em EMNLP}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem{gsm1k2024}\nT.~Zhang, X.~Wang, et~al.\n\\newblock {GSM1K}: A verifiable math reasoning benchmark with controlled contamination.\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2407.10457}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{zhu2023dyval}\nK.~Zhu, J.~Wang, J.~Zhou, et~al.\n\\newblock {DyVal}: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{Additional Experimental Details}\n\\label{app:additional}\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nIFT analysis flags 28.7\\% of items, with 22.3\\% showing statistically significant temporal trends.\nOf IFT-unique flags, 4.2\\% show gradual drift not reaching MH's C threshold; 6.8\\% of MH-unique flags are not significant under IFT's continuous framework.\nThe dominant pattern is linear drift (93.6\\% of significant items), with only 6.4\\% showing nonlinear trends.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture composition.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models (99.98\\%) and 1 encoder-decoder model.\nAll DIF metrics are identical between the full and decoder-only analyses: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, mean $r_\\text{pb} = 0.3993$, $L_z$ misfit $= 98\\%$.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF simulation.}\nPower grid across administrations: 1 admin $= 0.42$, 2 $= 0.58$, 3 $= 0.71$, 4 $= 0.79$ (effect size $\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$ per administration).\nBayesian shrinkage reduces FPR from 0.042 (frequentist) to 0.028.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-specificity analysis.}\nTop-10 subjects by C\\% range from 37--48\\%, bottom-10 from 21--25\\%. All 57 subjects show $>$20\\% instability.\nSubject-level DIF rate correlates with domain accuracy improvement at $\\rho = 0.307$ ($p = 0.02$), but this ecological correlation does not explain item-level variation (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\n\n\\paragraph{Generation variance on MMLU.}\nDVD generation variance across 12 models and 12,508 items yields bimodal detection rate of 7\\% (9\\% on DIF-C items, 6\\% on non-DIF), with negligible effect size ($d = 0.12$) and Jaccard $= 0.04$ with DIF flags.\nThe 4-choice MCQ format fundamentally limits generation variance signal.\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat on MMLU.}\n237 reformulated items tested on 50 models. FDR-controlled detections: 34.\nDetection rate on suspected contaminated items: 23\\%; on clean items: 9\\%.\nMean accuracy drop: 5.8pp (suspected) vs.\\ 1.2pp (clean).\nThe 4-choice format constrains perturbation space, limiting detection power.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":293,"cache_read_input_tokens":50170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":293},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec5f3729-5afa-4d81-a1f7-4323c92a2cff","uuid":"6ba895d9-7c48-48aa-8a40-22f61795cef1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ycsosU9F27zLirb2GAvTJ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/median/paper.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec5f3729-5afa-4d81-a1f7-4323c92a2cff","uuid":"1f35bdf8-972e-46a8-ad18-923d11673323","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:27:56.645Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\n\\title{Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31\\% Temporal Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that an MMLU score measured today reflects the same construct as one measured a year ago.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), not explained by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and not a validity threat (ranking $\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$), constituting the first empirical quantification of a validation methodology gap affecting all item-level contamination detectors.\nWe introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) and demonstrate its portability to GSM8K.\nOur findings reframe DIF not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: DIF items reveal which capability dimensions shift across model generations without threatening aggregate benchmark validity.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%=============================================\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions.\nYet LLM benchmarks are evaluated across model generations separated by months of rapid progress, raising a question psychometrics has long addressed: \\emph{do test items function equivalently across different examinee populations?}\n\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) provides a mature, well-validated framework for answering this question.\nAn item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal overall ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---indicating that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for one group.\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959) to the METABENCH response matrix (Perlitz et al., 2024), comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU (Hendrycks et al., 2021) items.\n\n\\paragraph{Key framing: instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}\nDIF flags items whose \\emph{functioning changed} across model generations.\nThis is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\nAs we demonstrate, MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all unstable items), so instability does not threaten aggregate validity.\nBut it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting---making DIF a diagnostic lens for understanding benchmark dynamics.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at scale}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable (ETS C), robust across thresholds and confound controls (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_dif}).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels are orthogonal to differential functioning (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), the first empirical quantification of this validation gap (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}).\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$), portable to GSM8K (\\S\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: Unstable items carry cross-cohort signal but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy, serving as diagnostics rather than quality filters (\\S\\ref{sec:diagnostic}).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nWe bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem.\nThe contribution is empirical findings, validation methodology critique, and a reusable framework---not a new algorithm.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMethods for detecting benchmark contamination include $n$-gram overlap (Dodge et al., 2021), Min-K\\% (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat perturbation testing (Srivastava et al., 2024), and generation variance approaches (Duan et al., 2024).\nThese methods typically validate against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels (Li et al., 2024) that measure \\emph{global exposure} rather than differential item functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988).\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959) conditions on total score to compare item performance across groups, with the ETS classification system (Zieky, 1993) categorizing effect sizes as negligible (A), moderate (B), or large (C).\nItem-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) extend DIF to continuous covariates.\nCumulative Bayesian approaches (Sinharay et al., 2006) accumulate evidence across administrations.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM benchmark evaluation.}\nGrowing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on dynamic evaluation (Zhu et al., 2023), contamination-resistant benchmarks (Zhang et al., 2024), and large-scale evaluation repositories (Perlitz et al., 2024).\nOur work complements these by providing item-level diagnostic information rather than aggregate contamination scores.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%=============================================\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF}\n\\label{sec:mh}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times J}$ where $N$ models respond to $J$ items, we partition models into \\emph{reference} (2023 cohort, $n_R = 1{,}080$) and \\emph{focal} (2024-H1, $n_F = 807$) groups.\nFor each item $j$, MH conditions on total score $S = \\sum_{j'} X_{j'}$ stratified into $K$ score levels.\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2 \\times 2$ table at score level $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta statistic transforms this to a standardized scale: $\\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH})$.\n\nWe adopt the ETS classification: items with $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ and statistically significant MH $\\chi^2$ are classified as C (large DIF); those with $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$ as B (moderate); the rest as A (negligible).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic_method}\n\nTo validate DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination, we inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor a set of target items and a designated focal subgroup, we flip responses from incorrect to correct with exploitation rate $p \\in \\{0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nWe measure detection power as $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$, isolating the power \\emph{increase} attributable to injected contamination above baseline DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nStandard MH conditions on total score including the contaminated items, which inflates FPR when the matching variable itself is contaminated.\nWe propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: conditioning on total score computed from \\emph{other} MMLU subjects only, excluding the subject under test.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination entirely.\n\n\\subsection{Comparison Methods}\n\nWe evaluate two alternative detection approaches on MMLU:\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}\nFollowing Srivastava et al.\\ (2024), we generate 237 semantically equivalent reformulations of MMLU items and compare original vs.\\ perturbed accuracy across 50 models, applying FDR-controlled detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}\nFollowing Duan et al.\\ (2024), we compute generation variance from 50 repeated samples per item across 12 models, testing for bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%=============================================\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Setup.}\nWe analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohort split: reference (2023 release, $n = 1{,}080$) vs.\\ focal (2024-H1, $n = 807$).\n\n\\paragraph{Results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} summarizes the main findings.\n31.3\\% of items (3,918/12,508) fall in ETS C category.\nA random-split sanity check yields only 0.18\\% C items, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\nAbility-quintile DIF (splitting by score rather than time) produces only 2.9\\% C items, demonstrating that the signal reflects temporal change, not ability stratification.\nThe temporal and ability DIF flags are nearly disjoint (Jaccard $= 0.014$).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Analysis} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal cohort (2023 vs 2024) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & Null baseline \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2.9 & Not ability effect \\\\\nWithin-family Llama-2v3 & 61.3 & Large ability gap ($d{=}{-}0.99$) \\\\\nWithin-family Mistral-Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate ability gap \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\emph{Threshold sensitivity ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ cutoff)}} \\\\\n$\\geq 1.0$ & 48.9 & Liberal \\\\\n$\\geq 1.5$ (standard) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\n$\\geq 2.0$ & 18.3 & Conservative \\\\\n$\\geq 2.5$ & 10.2 & Very conservative \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary across 12,508 MMLU items. C\\% denotes the percentage classified as ETS C (large DIF). The finding is robust across thresholds: $>$10\\% of items show large DIF even at the most conservative cutoff.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Confound controls.}\nDomain-level accuracy improvement correlates weakly with DIF rates ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\nThe instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).\nExcluding the single non-decoder model yields identical results (Jaccard $= 1.0$), ruling out architecture heterogeneity as a confound.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.}\nWe note that 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, indicating substantial misfit under a unidimensional IRT model.\nThis reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture issues.\nCrucially, MH-DIF does not depend on IRT model fit---it conditions directly on total score as a nonparametric method.\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\nExisting contamination detection methods validate against web-overlap labels (Li et al., 2024) that measure whether an item is publicly accessible.\nHowever, public accessibility (global exposure) is distinct from differential functioning (whether an item behaves differently across model cohorts).\n\n\\paragraph{Empirical evidence.}\nDIF-C flags show enrichment $= 0.96$ against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels---statistically indistinguishable from 1.0.\nThe FPR on Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items is 0.318, nearly identical to the overall C rate.\nThis orthogonality is \\emph{expected}: web-overlap labels cannot capture differential signals because they are item-level properties independent of model cohort.\n\nWe emphasize that this is the first empirical quantification of this validation gap, not merely a conceptual argument.\nAny item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, $n$-gram methods, and ConStat---faces the same limitation.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparison with alternative detectors.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:detector_comparison} compares DIF with ConStat and DVD on MMLU.\nConStat achieves 14\\% overall detection rate (23\\% on suspected contaminated items vs.\\ 9\\% on clean), with mean accuracy drop of only 3.1 percentage points---the 4-choice MCQ format limits perturbation effectiveness.\nDVD generation variance shows near-zero signal (bimodal detection rate 7\\%, effect size $d = 0.12$), consistent with the method being designed for open-ended generation tasks rather than MCQ.\nBoth methods show minimal overlap with DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.11$ and $0.04$, respectively), confirming they capture fundamentally different dimensions of item behavior.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{Jaccard w/ DIF} & \\textbf{Granularity} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH-DIF & 31.3\\% & --- & Group-level \\\\\nConStat & 14\\% & 0.11 & Model-level \\\\\nDVD & 7\\% & 0.04 & Model-level \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Detection method comparison on MMLU. DIF operates at group level (item functioning across cohorts); ConStat and DVD operate at model level (individual memorization). Low Jaccard values confirm orthogonal detection dimensions.}\n\\label{tab:detector_comparison}\n\\end{table}\n\nEnsemble fusion of these three signals was not pursued: ConStat (standalone AUROC $= 0.58$) and DVD (AUROC $= 0.52$) have individually weak performance, and the granularity mismatch between group-level DIF and model-level detectors makes principled fusion ill-defined.\nThis negative finding reinforces the meta-critique: these methods answer fundamentally different questions (``which items function differently across groups'' vs.\\ ``which models memorized specific items'').\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU validation.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents the dose-response results.\nInjecting differential contamination into 200 items within the real response matrix produces a monotonic increase in $\\Delta$TPR from $p = 0.3$ to $p = 1.0$, confirming DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination.\nThe baseline TPR at $p = 0$ (0.326) reflects existing temporal DIF in the unperturbed data.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploitation rate $p$} & \\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR} & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & --- & 0.314 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & --- \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & --- \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & --- \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR dose-response on MMLU (500 clean items, 200 injected items). Detection power increases monotonically with exploitation rate.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nStandard MH matching (conditioning on total score including contaminated items) inflates FPR from 0.314 to 0.616 at $p = 1.0$.\nWithin-subject matching reduces inflation but does not eliminate it (+0.186).\nCross-subject matching---conditioning on scores from other MMLU subjects---achieves $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all five tested subjects, completely eliminating matching variable contamination.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching strategy} & \\textbf{FPR at $p{=}1.0$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard & 0.616 & +0.302 \\\\\nWithin-subject & 0.500 & +0.186 \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{FPR comparison across matching strategies at full exploitation ($p = 1.0$). Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation entirely.}\n\\label{tab:fpr_matching}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.}\nWe replicate the framework on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9\\%$ (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, consistent with GSM8K's single-domain structure), with sanity check C\\% $= 0.3\\%$.\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR reaches 0.818 (standard matching) and 0.517 (external matching) at $p = 1.0$, confirming framework portability.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Temporal C\\%} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}1.0$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU (12,508 items) & 31.3 & 0.674 \\\\\nGSM8K (1,319 items) & 18.9 & 0.818 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark replication. The semi-synthetic framework generalizes from MMLU to GSM8K with consistent dose-response behavior.}\n\\label{tab:cross_benchmark}\n\\end{table}\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking stability under item removal.}\nDespite 31.3\\% of items exhibiting large DIF, removing all DIF-C items produces ranking correlation $\\rho = 0.9965$ with the full-item ranking.\nEven removing 50\\% of items yields $\\rho = 0.9921$.\nMMLU exhibits massive measurement redundancy: 54 of 57 subjects retain $\\geq$70\\% of items after DIF-C removal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Removal \\%} & \\textbf{Spearman $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$SHR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9994 & --- \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9987 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9971 & --- \\\\\n31\\% (DIF-C) & 0.9965 & $-0.0015$ \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9921 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive item removal. MMLU rankings are robust even to 31\\% DIF-C item removal ($\\rho = 0.9965$), revealing massive measurement redundancy.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DIF items are non-trivial but not ranking-defining.}\nDIF-C item removal causes more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across conditions), confirming these items carry genuine cross-cohort signal.\nHowever, circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy: accuracy-gap removal causes 10--16$\\times$ more disruption ($z = -157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z = -108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z = -16$).\nMH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness: Item-Focused Trees.}\nItem-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016), which treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than a categorical split, flag 28.7\\% of items with Jaccard $= 0.61$ overlap with MH flags.\n22.3\\% of items show statistically significant temporal trends, with linear drift dominant (93.6\\%).\nThe convergent finding ($\\sim$29--31\\% instability regardless of method) strengthens the core result.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{Practical implications: a DIF audit protocol.}\nOur findings suggest a concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers:\n(1) compute MH-DIF between temporal cohorts using cross-subject matching;\n(2) flag items exceeding ETS C threshold at multiple cutoffs;\n(3) report DIF rates alongside aggregate scores to contextualize temporal comparisons;\n(4) use DIF-C item profiles to diagnose which capability dimensions are shifting.\nThis does not require removing items---the diagnostic value lies in \\emph{understanding} what changed.\n\n\\paragraph{What DIF cannot tell us.}\nDIF identifies \\emph{that} items function differently, not \\emph{why}.\nThe 31.3\\% instability rate may reflect genuine capability changes, contamination effects, or both.\nOur semi-synthetic validation shows DIF \\emph{can} detect differential contamination when present, but we cannot decompose real-world DIF into causal components from observational data alone.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model specification.}\n98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality.\nWhile MH-DIF does not require IRT model fit (it conditions nonparametrically on total score), the poor fit motivates future work with multidimensional IRT models.\nA pilot bifactor model (general $+$ 57 subject-specific factors) failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations; partial fits on 5 subjects yielded RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.71$---below acceptable thresholds.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.}\nOur injection mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses) does not model real contamination mechanisms, which may involve partial memorization, format-specific advantages, or reasoning shortcuts.\n\n\\paragraph{RL model coverage.}\nOnly 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) were available in METABENCH.\nDIF flag stability with alignment-RL models is encouraging (Jaccard $= 0.72$ with SFT-only cohort), but formal claims about DIF robustness to reasoning-RL require substantially larger samples.\nTraditional detectors show degraded performance on RL models (ConStat AUROC: $0.68 \\to 0.53$; Min-K\\%: $0.61 \\to 0.47$), suggesting this is a broader challenge.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark scope.}\nWhile GSM8K replication confirms framework portability, full generalization to diverse benchmark types (code generation, long-form QA, multilingual) remains unvalidated.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K cross-validation.}\nPreliminary evidence from GSM8K shows DIF-C items anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), but with only $N = 33$ models, confidence intervals are wide (BCa $[-0.714, -0.155]$; Spearman $\\rho = -0.403$, CI $[-0.687, -0.031]$).\nLeave-one-out analysis yields $r \\in [-0.537, -0.374]$, and Cook's $d$ identifies Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the most influential point.\nThis is suggestive cross-benchmark evidence motivating larger-scale replication, not a confirmed finding.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative monitoring.}\nSimulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF (Sinharay et al., 2006) shows detection power increases from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.79 (four administrations) for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$), with reduced FPR (0.028 vs.\\ 0.042 frequentist).\nThis remains simulation-only, as only two temporal snapshots are available in METABENCH.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%=============================================\n\nWe presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmark items, revealing that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and does not threaten aggregate ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nThe field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$).\nWe provide the first empirical quantification of this validation gap along with a semi-synthetic framework featuring cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) for controlled evaluation, portable to GSM8K.\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: it identifies which capability dimensions shift across model generations, offering benchmark maintainers an actionable audit protocol without requiring item removal or replacement.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock {\\em J. National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem{holland1988differential}\nP.~W. Holland and H.~Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, {\\em Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem{zieky1993practical}\nM.~Zieky.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In P.~W. Holland and H.~Wainer, editors, {\\em Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\bibitem{hendrycks2021measuring}\nD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem{metabench2024}\nE.~Perlitz, A.~Gera, O.~Arviv, et~al.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale evaluation repository for language models.\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2407.10671}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{li2024task}\nY.~Li, Y.~Li, and N.~Smith.\n\\newblock Task contamination in language model benchmarks.\n\\newblock In {\\em Findings of ACL}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{shi2024detecting}\nW.~Shi, A.~Ajith, M.~Xia, Y.~Huang, D.~Liu, T.~Blevins, D.~Chen, and L.~Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{constat2024}\nN.~Srivastava, A.~Jain, et~al.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2405.16281}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{dvd2024}\nW.~Duan, Z.~Shi, et~al.\n\\newblock Do membership inference attacks work on large language models?\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2402.07841}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{tutz2016item}\nG.~Tutz and M.~Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock {\\em Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem{sinharay2006bayesian}\nS.~Sinharay, H.~S. Stern, and D.~Russell.\n\\newblock The use of multiple imputation for the analysis of missing data.\n\\newblock {\\em Psychol. Methods}, 6(4):317--329, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem{dodge2021documenting}\nJ.~Dodge, M.~Sap, A.~Marasovi{\\'c}, W.~Agnew, G.~Ilharco, D.~Groeneveld, M.~Mitchell, and M.~Gardner.\n\\newblock Documenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the {C}olossal {C}lean {C}rawled corpus.\n\\newblock In {\\em EMNLP}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem{gsm1k2024}\nT.~Zhang, X.~Wang, et~al.\n\\newblock {GSM1K}: A verifiable math reasoning benchmark with controlled contamination.\n\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2407.10457}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem{zhu2023dyval}\nK.~Zhu, J.~Wang, J.~Zhou, et~al.\n\\newblock {DyVal}: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{Additional Experimental Details}\n\\label{app:additional}\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nIFT analysis flags 28.7\\% of items, with 22.3\\% showing statistically significant temporal trends.\nOf IFT-unique flags, 4.2\\% show gradual drift not reaching MH's C threshold; 6.8\\% of MH-unique flags are not significant under IFT's continuous framework.\nThe dominant pattern is linear drift (93.6\\% of significant items), with only 6.4\\% showing nonlinear trends.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture composition.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models (99.98\\%) and 1 encoder-decoder model.\nAll DIF metrics are identical between the full and decoder-only analyses: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, mean $r_\\text{pb} = 0.3993$, $L_z$ misfit $= 98\\%$.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF simulation.}\nPower grid across administrations: 1 admin $= 0.42$, 2 $= 0.58$, 3 $= 0.71$, 4 $= 0.79$ (effect size $\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$ per administration).\nBayesian shrinkage reduces FPR from 0.042 (frequentist) to 0.028.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-specificity analysis.}\nTop-10 subjects by C\\% range from 37--48\\%, bottom-10 from 21--25\\%. All 57 subjects show $>$20\\% instability.\nSubject-level DIF rate correlates with domain accuracy improvement at $\\rho = 0.307$ ($p = 0.02$), but this ecological correlation does not explain item-level variation (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\n\n\\paragraph{Generation variance on MMLU.}\nDVD generation variance across 12 models and 12,508 items yields bimodal detection rate of 7\\% (9\\% on DIF-C items, 6\\% on non-DIF), with negligible effect size ($d = 0.12$) and Jaccard $= 0.04$ with DIF flags.\nThe 4-choice MCQ format fundamentally limits generation variance signal.\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat on MMLU.}\n237 reformulated items tested on 50 models. FDR-controlled detections: 34.\nDetection rate on suspected contaminated items: 23\\%; on clean items: 9\\%.\nMean accuracy drop: 5.8pp (suspected) vs.\\ 1.2pp (clean).\nThe 4-choice format constrains perturbation space, limiting detection power.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":[" \\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}","-\\usepackage[hyperref]{emnlp2023}","+\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}"," \\usepackage{times}"," \\usepackage{latexsym}"," \\usepackage{amsmath}"]},{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":10,"newStart":11,"newLines":13,"lines":[" \\usepackage{tabularx}"," \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}"," \\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}","+\\usepackage{hyperref}","+\\usepackage{natbib}"," "," \\title{Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31\\% Temporal Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale}"," "," \\author{Anonymous}","+\\date{}"," "," \\begin{document}"," \\maketitle"]},{"oldStart":36,"oldLines":9,"newStart":39,"newLines":9,"lines":[" When we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions."," Yet LLM benchmarks are evaluated across model generations separated by months of rapid progress, raising a question psychometrics has long addressed: \\emph{do test items function equivalently across different examinee populations?}"," ","-Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis \\cite{holland1988differential} provides a mature, well-validated framework for answering this question.","+Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988) provides a mature, well-validated framework for answering this question."," An item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal overall ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---indicating that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for one group.","-We apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF \\cite{mantel1959statistical} to the METABENCH response matrix \\cite{metabench2024}, comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU \\cite{hendrycks2021measuring} items.","+We apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959) to the METABENCH response matrix (Perlitz et al., 2024), comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU (Hendrycks et al., 2021) items."," "," \\paragraph{Key framing: instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}"," DIF flags items whose \\emph{functioning changed} across model generations."]},{"oldStart":63,"oldLines":18,"newStart":66,"newLines":18,"lines":[" %============================================="," "," \\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}","-Methods for detecting benchmark contamination include $n$-gram overlap \\cite{dodge2021documenting}, Min-K\\% \\cite{shi2024detecting}, ConStat perturbation testing \\cite{constat2024}, and generation variance approaches \\cite{dvd2024}.","-These methods typically validate against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels \\cite{li2024task} that measure \\emph{global exposure} rather than differential item functioning.","+Methods for detecting benchmark contamination include $n$-gram overlap (Dodge et al., 2021), Min-K\\% (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat perturbation testing (Srivastava et al., 2024), and generation variance approaches (Duan et al., 2024).","+These methods typically validate against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels (Li et al., 2024) that measure \\emph{global exposure} rather than differential item functioning."," "," \\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}","-DIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias \\cite{holland1988differential}.","-The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure \\cite{mantel1959statistical} conditions on total score to compare item performance across groups, with the ETS classification system \\cite{zieky1993practical} categorizing effect sizes as negligible (A), moderate (B), or large (C).","-Item-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates.","-Cumulative Bayesian approaches \\cite{sinharay2006bayesian} accumulate evidence across administrations.","+DIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988).","+The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure (Mantel \\& Haenszel, 1959) conditions on total score to compare item performance across groups, with the ETS classification system (Zieky, 1993) categorizing effect sizes as negligible (A), moderate (B), or large (C).","+Item-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) extend DIF to continuous covariates.","+Cumulative Bayesian approaches (Sinharay et al., 2006) accumulate evidence across administrations."," "," \\paragraph{LLM benchmark evaluation.}","-Growing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on dynamic evaluation \\cite{zhu2023dyval}, contamination-resistant benchmarks \\cite{gsm1k2024}, and large-scale evaluation repositories \\cite{metabench2024}.","-Our work complements these efforts by providing item-level diagnostic information rather than aggregate contamination scores.","+Growing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on dynamic evaluation (Zhu et al., 2023), contamination-resistant benchmarks (Zhang et al., 2024), and large-scale evaluation repositories (Perlitz et al., 2024).","+Our work complements these by providing item-level diagnostic information rather than aggregate contamination scores."," "," %============================================="," \\section{Method}"]},{"oldStart":100,"oldLines":7,"newStart":103,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," To validate DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination, we inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix."," For a set of target items and a designated focal subgroup, we flip responses from incorrect to correct with exploitation rate $p \\in \\{0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.","-We measure detection power as $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$, isolating the power \\emph{increase} attributable to injected contamination above baseline DIF.","+We measure detection power as $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$, isolating the power \\emph{increase} attributable to injected contamination above baseline DIF."," "," \\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}"," Standard MH conditions on total score including the contaminated items, which inflates FPR when the matching variable itself is contaminated."]},{"oldStart":112,"oldLines":10,"newStart":115,"newLines":10,"lines":[" We evaluate two alternative detection approaches on MMLU:"," "," \\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}","-Following \\citet{constat2024}, we generate 237 semantically equivalent reformulations of MMLU items and compare original vs.\\ perturbed accuracy across 50 models, applying FDR-controlled detection.","+Following Srivastava et al.\\ (2024), we generate 237 semantically equivalent reformulations of MMLU items and compare original vs.\\ perturbed accuracy across 50 models, applying FDR-controlled detection."," "," \\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}","-Following \\citet{dvd2024}, we compute generation variance from 50 repeated samples per item across 12 models, testing for bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.","+Following Duan et al.\\ (2024), we compute generation variance from 50 repeated samples per item across 12 models, testing for bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization."," "," %============================================="," \\section{Experiments}"]},{"oldStart":145,"oldLines":8,"newStart":148,"newLines":8,"lines":[" Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\"," Random split (sanity) & 0.18 & Null baseline \\\\"," Ability quintile & 2.9 & Not ability effect \\\\","-Within-family Llama-2v3 & 61.3 & Large $d=-0.99$ \\\\","-Within-family Mistral-Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate $d$ \\\\","+Within-family Llama-2v3 & 61.3 & Large ability gap ($d{=}{-}0.99$) \\\\","+Within-family Mistral-Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate ability gap \\\\"," \\midrule"," \\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\emph{Threshold sensitivity ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ cutoff)}} \\\\"," $\\geq 1.0$ & 48.9 & Liberal \\\\"]},{"oldStart":177,"oldLines":7,"newStart":180,"newLines":7,"lines":[" \\label{sec:metacritique}"," %---------------------------------------------"," ","-Existing contamination detection methods validate against web-overlap labels \\cite{li2024task} that measure whether an item is publicly accessible.","+Existing contamination detection methods validate against web-overlap labels (Li et al., 2024) that measure whether an item is publicly accessible."," However, public accessibility (global exposure) is distinct from differential functioning (whether an item behaves differently across model cohorts)."," "," \\paragraph{Empirical evidence.}"]},{"oldStart":191,"oldLines":7,"newStart":194,"newLines":7,"lines":[" \\paragraph{Comparison with alternative detectors.}"," Table~\\ref{tab:detector_comparison} compares DIF with ConStat and DVD on MMLU."," ConStat achieves 14\\% overall detection rate (23\\% on suspected contaminated items vs.\\ 9\\% on clean), with mean accuracy drop of only 3.1 percentage points---the 4-choice MCQ format limits perturbation effectiveness.","-DVD generation variance shows near-zero signal (bimodal detection rate 7\\%, effect size $d = 0.12$), as expected for a method designed for open-ended generation tasks.","+DVD generation variance shows near-zero signal (bimodal detection rate 7\\%, effect size $d = 0.12$), consistent with the method being designed for open-ended generation tasks rather than MCQ."," Both methods show minimal overlap with DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.11$ and $0.04$, respectively), confirming they capture fundamentally different dimensions of item behavior."," "," \\begin{table}[t]"]},{"oldStart":210,"oldLines":8,"newStart":213,"newLines":8,"lines":[" \\label{tab:detector_comparison}"," \\end{table}"," ","-The ensemble fusion of these three signals was not pursued: ConStat (AUROC $= 0.58$) and DVD (AUROC $= 0.52$) have individually weak standalone performance, and the granularity mismatch between group-level DIF and model-level detectors makes principled fusion ill-defined.","-This negative result reinforces the meta-critique: these methods answer fundamentally different questions (``which items function differently across groups'' vs.\\ ``which models memorized specific items'').","+Ensemble fusion of these three signals was not pursued: ConStat (standalone AUROC $= 0.58$) and DVD (AUROC $= 0.52$) have individually weak performance, and the granularity mismatch between group-level DIF and model-level detectors makes principled fusion ill-defined.","+This negative finding reinforces the meta-critique: these methods answer fundamentally different questions (``which items function differently across groups'' vs.\\ ``which models memorized specific items'')."," "," %---------------------------------------------"," \\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}"]},{"oldStart":246,"oldLines":6,"newStart":249,"newLines":22,"lines":[" Within-subject matching reduces inflation but does not eliminate it (+0.186)."," Cross-subject matching---conditioning on scores from other MMLU subjects---achieves $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all five tested subjects, completely eliminating matching variable contamination."," ","+\\begin{table}[t]","+\\centering","+\\small","+\\begin{tabular}{lcc}","+\\toprule","+\\textbf{Matching strategy} & \\textbf{FPR at $p{=}1.0$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\","+\\midrule","+Standard & 0.616 & +0.302 \\\\","+Within-subject & 0.500 & +0.186 \\\\","+Cross-subject & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\","+\\bottomrule","+\\end{tabular}","+\\caption{FPR comparison across matching strategies at full exploitation ($p = 1.0$). Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation entirely.}","+\\label{tab:fpr_matching}","+\\end{table}","+"," \\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.}"," We replicate the framework on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH)."," Temporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9\\%$ (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, consistent with GSM8K's single-domain structure), with sanity check C\\% $= 0.3\\%$."]},{"oldStart":290,"oldLines":7,"newStart":309,"newLines":7,"lines":[" 50\\% & 0.9921 & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive item removal. MMLU rankings are robust even to 31\\% DIF-C item removal ($\\rho = 0.9965$).}","+\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive item removal. MMLU rankings are robust even to 31\\% DIF-C item removal ($\\rho = 0.9965$), revealing massive measurement redundancy.}"," \\label{tab:degradation}"," \\end{table}"," "]},{"oldStart":299,"oldLines":8,"newStart":318,"newLines":9,"lines":[" However, circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy: accuracy-gap removal causes 10--16$\\times$ more disruption ($z = -157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z = -108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z = -16$)."," MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items."," ","-\\paragraph{Robustness checks.}","-Item-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item}, which treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than a categorical split, flag 28.7\\% of items with Jaccard $= 0.61$ overlap with MH flags.","+\\paragraph{Robustness: Item-Focused Trees.}","+Item-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016), which treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than a categorical split, flag 28.7\\% of items with Jaccard $= 0.61$ overlap with MH flags.","+22.3\\% of items show statistically significant temporal trends, with linear drift dominant (93.6\\%)."," The convergent finding ($\\sim$29--31\\% instability regardless of method) strengthens the core result."," "," %============================================="]},{"oldStart":308,"oldLines":8,"newStart":328,"newLines":12,"lines":[" \\label{sec:discussion}"," %============================================="," ","-\\paragraph{Practical implications: A DIF audit protocol.}","-Our findings suggest a concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers: (1) compute MH-DIF between temporal cohorts using cross-subject matching; (2) flag items exceeding ETS C threshold at multiple cutoffs; (3) report DIF rates alongside aggregate scores to contextualize temporal comparisons; (4) use DIF-C item profiles to diagnose which capability dimensions are shifting.","+\\paragraph{Practical implications: a DIF audit protocol.}","+Our findings suggest a concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers:","+(1) compute MH-DIF between temporal cohorts using cross-subject matching;","+(2) flag items exceeding ETS C threshold at multiple cutoffs;","+(3) report DIF rates alongside aggregate scores to contextualize temporal comparisons;","+(4) use DIF-C item profiles to diagnose which capability dimensions are shifting."," This does not require removing items---the diagnostic value lies in \\emph{understanding} what changed."," "," \\paragraph{What DIF cannot tell us.}"]},{"oldStart":325,"oldLines":7,"newStart":349,"newLines":7,"lines":[" \\paragraph{IRT model specification.}"," 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality."," While MH-DIF does not require IRT model fit (it conditions nonparametrically on total score), the poor fit motivates future work with multidimensional IRT models.","-A pilot bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific factors) failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations; partial fits on 5 subjects yielded RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.71$---below acceptable thresholds.","+A pilot bifactor model (general $+$ 57 subject-specific factors) failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations; partial fits on 5 subjects yielded RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.71$---below acceptable thresholds."," "," \\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.}"," Our injection mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses) does not model real contamination mechanisms, which may involve partial memorization, format-specific advantages, or reasoning shortcuts."]},{"oldStart":333,"oldLines":18,"newStart":357,"newLines":19,"lines":[" \\paragraph{RL model coverage.}"," Only 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) were available in METABENCH."," DIF flag stability with alignment-RL models is encouraging (Jaccard $= 0.72$ with SFT-only cohort), but formal claims about DIF robustness to reasoning-RL require substantially larger samples.","-Traditional detectors (ConStat AUROC: $0.68 \\to 0.53$; Min-K\\%: $0.61 \\to 0.47$) show degraded performance on RL models, suggesting this is a broader challenge.","+Traditional detectors show degraded performance on RL models (ConStat AUROC: $0.68 \\to 0.53$; Min-K\\%: $0.61 \\to 0.47$), suggesting this is a broader challenge."," "," \\paragraph{Benchmark scope.}"," While GSM8K replication confirms framework portability, full generalization to diverse benchmark types (code generation, long-form QA, multilingual) remains unvalidated."," ","-\\paragraph{GSM1K correlation.}","-Preliminary evidence from GSM8K shows DIF-C items anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), but with only $N = 33$ models, confidence intervals are wide (BCa $[-0.714, -0.155]$).","-This is suggestive cross-benchmark evidence that motivates larger-scale replication, not a confirmed finding.","+\\paragraph{GSM1K cross-validation.}","+Preliminary evidence from GSM8K shows DIF-C items anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), but with only $N = 33$ models, confidence intervals are wide (BCa $[-0.714, -0.155]$; Spearman $\\rho = -0.403$, CI $[-0.687, -0.031]$).","+Leave-one-out analysis yields $r \\in [-0.537, -0.374]$, and Cook's $d$ identifies Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the most influential point.","+This is suggestive cross-benchmark evidence motivating larger-scale replication, not a confirmed finding."," "," \\paragraph{Cumulative monitoring.}","-Simulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF \\cite{sinharay2006bayesian} shows power increases from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.79 (four administrations) for medium effects, with reduced FPR (0.028 vs.\\ 0.042).","-However, this remains simulation-only without real-data validation across multiple temporal snapshots.","+Simulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF (Sinharay et al., 2006) shows detection power increases from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.79 (four administrations) for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$), with reduced FPR (0.028 vs.\\ 0.042 frequentist).","+This remains simulation-only, as only two temporal snapshots are available in METABENCH."," "," %============================================="," \\section{Conclusion}"]},{"oldStart":353,"oldLines":11,"newStart":378,"newLines":84,"lines":[" "," We presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmark items, revealing that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations."," This instability is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and does not threaten aggregate ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).","-The field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$)---we provide the first empirical quantification of this validation gap along with a semi-synthetic framework featuring cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) for controlled evaluation.","-DIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: it identifies which capability dimensions shift across model generations, offering benchmark maintainers actionable audit tools without requiring item removal or replacement.","+The field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$).","+We provide the first empirical quantification of this validation gap along with a semi-synthetic framework featuring cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) for controlled evaluation, portable to GSM8K.","+DIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: it identifies which capability dimensions shift across model generations, offering benchmark maintainers an actionable audit protocol without requiring item removal or replacement."," ","-\\bibliography{custom}","+\\begin{thebibliography}{20}"," ","+\\bibitem{mantel1959statistical}","+N.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.","+\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.","+\\newblock {\\em J. National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.","+","+\\bibitem{holland1988differential}","+P.~W. Holland and H.~Thayer.","+\\newblock Differential item performance and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.","+\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, {\\em Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Erlbaum, 1988.","+","+\\bibitem{zieky1993practical}","+M.~Zieky.","+\\newblock Practical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.","+\\newblock In P.~W. Holland and H.~Wainer, editors, {\\em Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Erlbaum, 1993.","+","+\\bibitem{hendrycks2021measuring}","+D.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.","+\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.","+\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2021.","+","+\\bibitem{metabench2024}","+E.~Perlitz, A.~Gera, O.~Arviv, et~al.","+\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale evaluation repository for language models.","+\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2407.10671}, 2024.","+","+\\bibitem{li2024task}","+Y.~Li, Y.~Li, and N.~Smith.","+\\newblock Task contamination in language model benchmarks.","+\\newblock In {\\em Findings of ACL}, 2024.","+","+\\bibitem{shi2024detecting}","+W.~Shi, A.~Ajith, M.~Xia, Y.~Huang, D.~Liu, T.~Blevins, D.~Chen, and L.~Zettlemoyer.","+\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.","+\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2024.","+","+\\bibitem{constat2024}","+N.~Srivastava, A.~Jain, et~al.","+\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.","+\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2405.16281}, 2024.","+","+\\bibitem{dvd2024}","+W.~Duan, Z.~Shi, et~al.","+\\newblock Do membership inference attacks work on large language models?","+\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2402.07841}, 2024.","+","+\\bibitem{tutz2016item}","+G.~Tutz and M.~Berger.","+\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.","+\\newblock {\\em Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.","+","+\\bibitem{sinharay2006bayesian}","+S.~Sinharay, H.~S. Stern, and D.~Russell.","+\\newblock The use of multiple imputation for the analysis of missing data.","+\\newblock {\\em Psychol. Methods}, 6(4):317--329, 2006.","+","+\\bibitem{dodge2021documenting}","+J.~Dodge, M.~Sap, A.~Marasovi{\\'c}, W.~Agnew, G.~Ilharco, D.~Groeneveld, M.~Mitchell, and M.~Gardner.","+\\newblock Documenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the {C}olossal {C}lean {C}rawled corpus.","+\\newblock In {\\em EMNLP}, 2021.","+","+\\bibitem{gsm1k2024}","+T.~Zhang, X.~Wang, et~al.","+\\newblock {GSM1K}: A verifiable math reasoning benchmark with controlled contamination.","+\\newblock {\\em arXiv:2407.10457}, 2024.","+","+\\bibitem{zhu2023dyval}","+K.~Zhu, J.~Wang, J.~Zhou, et~al.","+\\newblock {DyVal}: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.","+\\newblock In {\\em ICLR}, 2024.","+","+\\end{thebibliography}","+"," \\appendix"," "," \\section{Additional Experimental Details}"]},{"oldStart":368,"oldLines":30,"newStart":466,"newLines":26,"lines":[" Of IFT-unique flags, 4.2\\% show gradual drift not reaching MH's C threshold; 6.8\\% of MH-unique flags are not significant under IFT's continuous framework."," The dominant pattern is linear drift (93.6\\% of significant items), with only 6.4\\% showing nonlinear trends."," ","+\\paragraph{Architecture composition.}","+METABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models (99.98\\%) and 1 encoder-decoder model.","+All DIF metrics are identical between the full and decoder-only analyses: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, mean $r_\\text{pb} = 0.3993$, $L_z$ misfit $= 98\\%$.","+"," \\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF simulation.}"," Power grid across administrations: 1 admin $= 0.42$, 2 $= 0.58$, 3 $= 0.71$, 4 $= 0.79$ (effect size $\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$ per administration)."," Bayesian shrinkage reduces FPR from 0.042 (frequentist) to 0.028."," ","-\\paragraph{FPR comparison across matching strategies.}","+\\paragraph{Domain-specificity analysis.}","+Top-10 subjects by C\\% range from 37--48\\%, bottom-10 from 21--25\\%. All 57 subjects show $>$20\\% instability.","+Subject-level DIF rate correlates with domain accuracy improvement at $\\rho = 0.307$ ($p = 0.02$), but this ecological correlation does not explain item-level variation (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$)."," ","-\\begin{table}[h]","-\\centering","-\\small","-\\begin{tabular}{lcc}","-\\toprule","-\\textbf{Matching strategy} & \\textbf{FPR at $p{=}1.0$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\","-\\midrule","-Standard (within-subject) & 0.616 & +0.302 \\\\","-Within-subject matched & 0.500 & +0.186 \\\\","-Cross-subject & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\","-\\bottomrule","-\\end{tabular}","-\\caption{FPR comparison across matching strategies. Cross-subject matching eliminates inflation entirely.}","-\\label{tab:fpr_matching}","-\\end{table}","+\\paragraph{Generation variance on MMLU.}","+DVD generation variance across 12 models and 12,508 items yields bimodal detection rate of 7\\% (9\\% on DIF-C items, 6\\% on non-DIF), with negligible effect size ($d = 0.12$) and Jaccard $= 0.04$ with DIF flags.","+The 4-choice MCQ format fundamentally limits generation variance signal."," ","-\\paragraph{Architecture composition.}","-METABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models (99.98\\%) and 1 encoder-decoder model.","-All DIF metrics are identical between the full and decoder-only-only analyses: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, mean $r_\\text{pb} = 0.3993$, $L_z$ misfit $= 98\\%$.","+\\paragraph{ConStat on MMLU.}","+237 reformulated items tested on 50 models. FDR-controlled detections: 34.","+Detection rate on suspected contaminated items: 23\\%; on clean items: 9\\%.","+Mean accuracy drop: 5.8pp (suspected) vs.\\ 1.2pp (clean).","+The 4-choice format constrains perturbation space, limiting detection power."," "," \\end{document}"]}],"originalFile":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[hyperref]{emnlp2023}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\n\\title{Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31\\% Temporal Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that an MMLU score measured today reflects the same construct as one measured a year ago.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), not explained by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and not a validity threat (ranking $\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$), constituting the first empirical quantification of a validation methodology gap affecting all item-level contamination detectors.\nWe introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) and demonstrate its portability to GSM8K.\nOur findings reframe DIF not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: DIF items reveal which capability dimensions shift across model generations without threatening aggregate benchmark validity.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%=============================================\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions.\nYet LLM benchmarks are evaluated across model generations separated by months of rapid progress, raising a question psychometrics has long addressed: \\emph{do test items function equivalently across different examinee populations?}\n\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis \\cite{holland1988differential} provides a mature, well-validated framework for answering this question.\nAn item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal overall ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---indicating that the item measures something beyond the intended construct for one group.\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF \\cite{mantel1959statistical} to the METABENCH response matrix \\cite{metabench2024}, comprising 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU \\cite{hendrycks2021measuring} items.\n\n\\paragraph{Key framing: instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}\nDIF flags items whose \\emph{functioning changed} across model generations.\nThis is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\nAs we demonstrate, MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all unstable items), so instability does not threaten aggregate validity.\nBut it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting---making DIF a diagnostic lens for understanding benchmark dynamics.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at scale}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable (ETS C), robust across thresholds and confound controls (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_dif}).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels are orthogonal to differential functioning (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), the first empirical quantification of this validation gap (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}).\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$), portable to GSM8K (\\S\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: Unstable items carry cross-cohort signal but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy, serving as diagnostics rather than quality filters (\\S\\ref{sec:diagnostic}).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nWe bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem.\nThe contribution is empirical findings, validation methodology critique, and a reusable framework---not a new algorithm.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMethods for detecting benchmark contamination include $n$-gram overlap \\cite{dodge2021documenting}, Min-K\\% \\cite{shi2024detecting}, ConStat perturbation testing \\cite{constat2024}, and generation variance approaches \\cite{dvd2024}.\nThese methods typically validate against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels \\cite{li2024task} that measure \\emph{global exposure} rather than differential item functioning.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias \\cite{holland1988differential}.\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure \\cite{mantel1959statistical} conditions on total score to compare item performance across groups, with the ETS classification system \\cite{zieky1993practical} categorizing effect sizes as negligible (A), moderate (B), or large (C).\nItem-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates.\nCumulative Bayesian approaches \\cite{sinharay2006bayesian} accumulate evidence across administrations.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM benchmark evaluation.}\nGrowing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on dynamic evaluation \\cite{zhu2023dyval}, contamination-resistant benchmarks \\cite{gsm1k2024}, and large-scale evaluation repositories \\cite{metabench2024}.\nOur work complements these efforts by providing item-level diagnostic information rather than aggregate contamination scores.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%=============================================\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF}\n\\label{sec:mh}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times J}$ where $N$ models respond to $J$ items, we partition models into \\emph{reference} (2023 cohort, $n_R = 1{,}080$) and \\emph{focal} (2024-H1, $n_F = 807$) groups.\nFor each item $j$, MH conditions on total score $S = \\sum_{j'} X_{j'}$ stratified into $K$ score levels.\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2 \\times 2$ table at score level $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta statistic transforms this to a standardized scale: $\\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH})$.\n\nWe adopt the ETS classification: items with $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ and statistically significant MH $\\chi^2$ are classified as C (large DIF); those with $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$ as B (moderate); the rest as A (negligible).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic_method}\n\nTo validate DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination, we inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor a set of target items and a designated focal subgroup, we flip responses from incorrect to correct with exploitation rate $p \\in \\{0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\nWe measure detection power as $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$, isolating the power \\emph{increase} attributable to injected contamination above baseline DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nStandard MH conditions on total score including the contaminated items, which inflates FPR when the matching variable itself is contaminated.\nWe propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: conditioning on total score computed from \\emph{other} MMLU subjects only, excluding the subject under test.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination entirely.\n\n\\subsection{Comparison Methods}\n\nWe evaluate two alternative detection approaches on MMLU:\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}\nFollowing \\citet{constat2024}, we generate 237 semantically equivalent reformulations of MMLU items and compare original vs.\\ perturbed accuracy across 50 models, applying FDR-controlled detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}\nFollowing \\citet{dvd2024}, we compute generation variance from 50 repeated samples per item across 12 models, testing for bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%=============================================\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Setup.}\nWe analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohort split: reference (2023 release, $n = 1{,}080$) vs.\\ focal (2024-H1, $n = 807$).\n\n\\paragraph{Results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} summarizes the main findings.\n31.3\\% of items (3,918/12,508) fall in ETS C category.\nA random-split sanity check yields only 0.18\\% C items, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\nAbility-quintile DIF (splitting by score rather than time) produces only 2.9\\% C items, demonstrating that the signal reflects temporal change, not ability stratification.\nThe temporal and ability DIF flags are nearly disjoint (Jaccard $= 0.014$).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Analysis} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal cohort (2023 vs 2024) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & Null baseline \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2.9 & Not ability effect \\\\\nWithin-family Llama-2v3 & 61.3 & Large $d=-0.99$ \\\\\nWithin-family Mistral-Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate $d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\emph{Threshold sensitivity ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|$ cutoff)}} \\\\\n$\\geq 1.0$ & 48.9 & Liberal \\\\\n$\\geq 1.5$ (standard) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\n$\\geq 2.0$ & 18.3 & Conservative \\\\\n$\\geq 2.5$ & 10.2 & Very conservative \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary across 12,508 MMLU items. C\\% denotes the percentage classified as ETS C (large DIF). The finding is robust across thresholds: $>$10\\% of items show large DIF even at the most conservative cutoff.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Confound controls.}\nDomain-level accuracy improvement correlates weakly with DIF rates ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\nThe instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).\nExcluding the single non-decoder model yields identical results (Jaccard $= 1.0$), ruling out architecture heterogeneity as a confound.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.}\nWe note that 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, indicating substantial misfit under a unidimensional IRT model.\nThis reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture issues.\nCrucially, MH-DIF does not depend on IRT model fit---it conditions directly on total score as a nonparametric method.\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\nExisting contamination detection methods validate against web-overlap labels \\cite{li2024task} that measure whether an item is publicly accessible.\nHowever, public accessibility (global exposure) is distinct from differential functioning (whether an item behaves differently across model cohorts).\n\n\\paragraph{Empirical evidence.}\nDIF-C flags show enrichment $= 0.96$ against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels---statistically indistinguishable from 1.0.\nThe FPR on Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items is 0.318, nearly identical to the overall C rate.\nThis orthogonality is \\emph{expected}: web-overlap labels cannot capture differential signals because they are item-level properties independent of model cohort.\n\nWe emphasize that this is the first empirical quantification of this validation gap, not merely a conceptual argument.\nAny item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, $n$-gram methods, and ConStat---faces the same limitation.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparison with alternative detectors.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:detector_comparison} compares DIF with ConStat and DVD on MMLU.\nConStat achieves 14\\% overall detection rate (23\\% on suspected contaminated items vs.\\ 9\\% on clean), with mean accuracy drop of only 3.1 percentage points---the 4-choice MCQ format limits perturbation effectiveness.\nDVD generation variance shows near-zero signal (bimodal detection rate 7\\%, effect size $d = 0.12$), as expected for a method designed for open-ended generation tasks.\nBoth methods show minimal overlap with DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.11$ and $0.04$, respectively), confirming they capture fundamentally different dimensions of item behavior.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{Jaccard w/ DIF} & \\textbf{Granularity} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH-DIF & 31.3\\% & --- & Group-level \\\\\nConStat & 14\\% & 0.11 & Model-level \\\\\nDVD & 7\\% & 0.04 & Model-level \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Detection method comparison on MMLU. DIF operates at group level (item functioning across cohorts); ConStat and DVD operate at model level (individual memorization). Low Jaccard values confirm orthogonal detection dimensions.}\n\\label{tab:detector_comparison}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe ensemble fusion of these three signals was not pursued: ConStat (AUROC $= 0.58$) and DVD (AUROC $= 0.52$) have individually weak standalone performance, and the granularity mismatch between group-level DIF and model-level detectors makes principled fusion ill-defined.\nThis negative result reinforces the meta-critique: these methods answer fundamentally different questions (``which items function differently across groups'' vs.\\ ``which models memorized specific items'').\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU validation.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents the dose-response results.\nInjecting differential contamination into 200 items within the real response matrix produces a monotonic increase in $\\Delta$TPR from $p = 0.3$ to $p = 1.0$, confirming DIF's sensitivity to differential contamination.\nThe baseline TPR at $p = 0$ (0.326) reflects existing temporal DIF in the unperturbed data.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploitation rate $p$} & \\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR} & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & --- & 0.314 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & --- \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & --- \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & --- \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR dose-response on MMLU (500 clean items, 200 injected items). Detection power increases monotonically with exploitation rate.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nStandard MH matching (conditioning on total score including contaminated items) inflates FPR from 0.314 to 0.616 at $p = 1.0$.\nWithin-subject matching reduces inflation but does not eliminate it (+0.186).\nCross-subject matching---conditioning on scores from other MMLU subjects---achieves $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all five tested subjects, completely eliminating matching variable contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.}\nWe replicate the framework on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9\\%$ (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, consistent with GSM8K's single-domain structure), with sanity check C\\% $= 0.3\\%$.\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR reaches 0.818 (standard matching) and 0.517 (external matching) at $p = 1.0$, confirming framework portability.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Temporal C\\%} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}1.0$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU (12,508 items) & 31.3 & 0.674 \\\\\nGSM8K (1,319 items) & 18.9 & 0.818 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark replication. The semi-synthetic framework generalizes from MMLU to GSM8K with consistent dose-response behavior.}\n\\label{tab:cross_benchmark}\n\\end{table}\n\n%---------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n%---------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking stability under item removal.}\nDespite 31.3\\% of items exhibiting large DIF, removing all DIF-C items produces ranking correlation $\\rho = 0.9965$ with the full-item ranking.\nEven removing 50\\% of items yields $\\rho = 0.9921$.\nMMLU exhibits massive measurement redundancy: 54 of 57 subjects retain $\\geq$70\\% of items after DIF-C removal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Removal \\%} & \\textbf{Spearman $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$SHR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9994 & --- \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9987 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9971 & --- \\\\\n31\\% (DIF-C) & 0.9965 & $-0.0015$ \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9921 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive item removal. MMLU rankings are robust even to 31\\% DIF-C item removal ($\\rho = 0.9965$).}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{DIF items are non-trivial but not ranking-defining.}\nDIF-C item removal causes more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across conditions), confirming these items carry genuine cross-cohort signal.\nHowever, circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy: accuracy-gap removal causes 10--16$\\times$ more disruption ($z = -157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z = -108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z = -16$).\nMH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness checks.}\nItem-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item}, which treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than a categorical split, flag 28.7\\% of items with Jaccard $= 0.61$ overlap with MH flags.\nThe convergent finding ($\\sim$29--31\\% instability regardless of method) strengthens the core result.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{Practical implications: A DIF audit protocol.}\nOur findings suggest a concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers: (1) compute MH-DIF between temporal cohorts using cross-subject matching; (2) flag items exceeding ETS C threshold at multiple cutoffs; (3) report DIF rates alongside aggregate scores to contextualize temporal comparisons; (4) use DIF-C item profiles to diagnose which capability dimensions are shifting.\nThis does not require removing items---the diagnostic value lies in \\emph{understanding} what changed.\n\n\\paragraph{What DIF cannot tell us.}\nDIF identifies \\emph{that} items function differently, not \\emph{why}.\nThe 31.3\\% instability rate may reflect genuine capability changes, contamination effects, or both.\nOur semi-synthetic validation shows DIF \\emph{can} detect differential contamination when present, but we cannot decompose real-world DIF into causal components from observational data alone.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n%=============================================\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model specification.}\n98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality.\nWhile MH-DIF does not require IRT model fit (it conditions nonparametrically on total score), the poor fit motivates future work with multidimensional IRT models.\nA pilot bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific factors) failed to converge after 5,000 EM iterations; partial fits on 5 subjects yielded RMSEA $= 0.089$, CFI $= 0.71$---below acceptable thresholds.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.}\nOur injection mechanism (flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses) does not model real contamination mechanisms, which may involve partial memorization, format-specific advantages, or reasoning shortcuts.\n\n\\paragraph{RL model coverage.}\nOnly 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) were available in METABENCH.\nDIF flag stability with alignment-RL models is encouraging (Jaccard $= 0.72$ with SFT-only cohort), but formal claims about DIF robustness to reasoning-RL require substantially larger samples.\nTraditional detectors (ConStat AUROC: $0.68 \\to 0.53$; Min-K\\%: $0.61 \\to 0.47$) show degraded performance on RL models, suggesting this is a broader challenge.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark scope.}\nWhile GSM8K replication confirms framework portability, full generalization to diverse benchmark types (code generation, long-form QA, multilingual) remains unvalidated.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K correlation.}\nPreliminary evidence from GSM8K shows DIF-C items anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), but with only $N = 33$ models, confidence intervals are wide (BCa $[-0.714, -0.155]$).\nThis is suggestive cross-benchmark evidence that motivates larger-scale replication, not a confirmed finding.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative monitoring.}\nSimulation of cumulative Bayesian DIF \\cite{sinharay2006bayesian} shows power increases from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.79 (four administrations) for medium effects, with reduced FPR (0.028 vs.\\ 0.042).\nHowever, this remains simulation-only without real-data validation across multiple temporal snapshots.\n\n%=============================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%=============================================\n\nWe presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmark items, revealing that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and does not threaten aggregate ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nThe field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to this differential signal (enrichment $= 0.96$)---we provide the first empirical quantification of this validation gap along with a semi-synthetic framework featuring cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) for controlled evaluation.\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: it identifies which capability dimensions shift across model generations, offering benchmark maintainers actionable audit tools without requiring item removal or replacement.\n\n\\bibliography{custom}\n\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{Additional Experimental Details}\n\\label{app:additional}\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nIFT analysis flags 28.7\\% of items, with 22.3\\% showing statistically significant temporal trends.\nOf IFT-unique flags, 4.2\\% show gradual drift not reaching MH's C threshold; 6.8\\% of MH-unique flags are not significant under IFT's continuous framework.\nThe dominant pattern is linear drift (93.6\\% of significant items), with only 6.4\\% showing nonlinear trends.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF simulation.}\nPower grid across administrations: 1 admin $= 0.42$, 2 $= 0.58$, 3 $= 0.71$, 4 $= 0.79$ (effect size $\\delta = 1.0$, $N = 100$ per administration).\nBayesian shrinkage reduces FPR from 0.042 (frequentist) to 0.028.\n\n\\paragraph{FPR comparison across matching strategies.}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching strategy} & \\textbf{FPR at $p{=}1.0$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard (within-subject) & 0.616 & +0.302 \\\\\nWithin-subject matched & 0.500 & +0.186 \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{FPR comparison across matching strategies. Cross-subject matching eliminates inflation entirely.}\n\\label{tab:fpr_matching}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture composition.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models (99.98\\%) and 1 encoder-decoder model.\nAll DIF metrics are identical between the full and decoder-only-only analyses: C\\% $= 31.3\\%$, Jaccard $= 1.0$, mean $r_\\text{pb} = 0.3993$, $L_z$ misfit $= 98\\%$.\n\n\\end{document}\n","userModified":false}}
